+# Moving the installed app to a new origin
+
+The origins are set in `migration.config.json`:
+
+| | Origin |
+|---|---|
+| Legacy (currently installed) | `https://dailygrind.example` |
+| New | `https://app.dailygrind.coffee` |
+
+After changing anything there, run `npm run build:migration`. It regenerates
+`manifest.webmanifest`, `manifest-legacy.webmanifest`,
+`.well-known/web-app-origin-association` and `js/origins.js`.
+
+## What moves, and how
+
+**The installation and OS shortcuts** (Start menu/dock entries, taskbar pins,
+jump-list/long-press shortcuts) move through the browser's web app migration:
+
+- The legacy origin serves `manifest-legacy.webmanifest`, which includes
+  `migrate_to` → the new app id and install URL.
+- The new origin serves `manifest.webmanifest`, which includes `migrate_from`
+  → the legacy app id. `behavior` is `"suggest"`; set it to `"force"` in the
+  config if you want the switch to happen without a prompt.
+- The legacy origin serves `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association`,
+  confirming the new app may take over the installation.
+- Both manifests use the same `id` path, name, icons and an identical
+  `shortcuts` list with the same relative URLs. Shortcuts the browser
+  recreates therefore open the same screens as before.
+
+**User configuration** (stored under `dg-prefs` in localStorage) can't follow
+an origin change, so `js/migration.js` hands it over itself. The data travels
+in the URL fragment, which is never sent to a server:
+
+- *Push*: a browser tab on the legacy origin redirects to the same path on the
+  new origin, carrying the user's preferences.
+- *Installed legacy app*: it does **not** redirect on its own. It stays put so
+  the browser can read `migrate_to` and run its own migration (that is the
+  step that keeps OS shortcuts). A banner offers to move right away.
+- *Pull*: when the migrated app first starts on the new origin with no
+  preferences, it makes one round trip to the legacy origin
+  (`?dg-export=<nonce>`) and comes back with them. This runs once.
+- Incoming data is checked against an allowlist and its timestamp is capped at
+  the current time. A payload the app didn't ask for can only fill an empty
+  profile; it never replaces existing settings. Return paths have to be
+  relative to the new origin, so the exporter can't be used as an open redirect.
+
+## Deployment notes
+
+- Deploy the same build to both origins. `js/origins.js` detects the legacy
+  origin and points the `<link rel="manifest">` at the legacy manifest. If
+  your host supports it, also rewrite `/manifest.webmanifest` →
+  `/manifest-legacy.webmanifest` on the legacy host so non-JS fetchers get it too.
+- The host must serve dot-directories so that
+  `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` is reachable, with
+  `Content-Type: application/json`.
+- Keep the legacy origin up until users have migrated. After that, set
+  `LEGACY_BRIDGE_ACTIVE = false` in `js/migration.js`.
